  "verbalization": "Betamethasone, a corticosteroid, is associated with Erythema Multiforme through a chain of indications that involve corticosteroids and their use in various conditions. The evidence suggests that Betamethasone may be linked to Erythema Multiforme via the use of hydrocortisone acetate, which is indicated in multiple conditions, including Erythema Multiforme. This pathway is supported by the indication of Betamethasone in Miliary Tuberculosis and Methylprednisolone, which in turn are linked to Erythema Multiforme. Similarly, Betamethasone is associated with Stevens-Johnson Syndrome, which is also linked to hydrocortisone acetate and Erythema Multiforme. The overlapping use of corticosteroids in these conditions implies a potential mechanism where corticosteroids may contribute to the development of Erythema Multiforme, possibly through immunomodulatory effects or inflammatory pathways. The evidence suggests a possible role of corticosteroids in the pathogenesis of Erythema Multiforme, though the exact mechanism remains to be elucidated.",
